      "page_title": "Command Substitution Fundamentals",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Core Move",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "`$(cmd)` runs `cmd` in a subshell and replaces itself with its stdout. `VAR=$(date)` captures it in one step."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "VAR=$(date)",
            "echo \"$VAR\"   # one string",
            "echo $VAR     # split into words"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "$() vs Backticks"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`$(...)` nests: `$(grep p $(find .))` reads inside-out",
            "Backticks demand nested backslash escapes",
            "Inside strings, `$( )` needs no special escaping"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "",
            "`$( )`",
            "backticks"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Nesting",
              "natural",
              "backslash hell"
            ],
            [
              "Strings",
              "no escapes",
              "escape inner"
            ],
            [
              "Verdict",
              "always",
              "legacy only"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "run cmd",
              "buffer stdout",
              "swap in text"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Splitting Rules"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Unquoted `$(ls)` splits at spaces, tabs, newlines",
            "Empty arguments vanish when unquoted",
            "Double-quote anything meant as one argument",
            "Quoted echo keeps newlines; unquoted loop splits"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "printf 'a\\n\\n\\n' > raw",
            "v=$(cat raw)",
            "echo \"[$v]\"    # [a] — tails stripped"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Subshell Side Effects",
          "items": [
            "`cd`, vars, `set -x` never leak out",
            "Inner status lost — capture `rc` separately",
            "Only stdout captured; stderr hits terminal"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Can I consume output while the command still runs?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "No — it runs to completion first",
            "Everything buffers before use",
            "Need streaming? Use a pipe"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "v=$(bigjob)  # waits for it all",
            "bigjob | while read l; do :; done"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Memory Model",
          "text": "Every byte is buffered before the outer command sees one. Unbounded output means unbounded RAM — capture small, stream big."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Parsing Trap",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "for f in $(ls); do    # breaks",
            "  echo \"$f\"",
            "done",
            "for f in *; do echo \"$f\"; done"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "find queue -type f -print0 |",
            "  xargs -0 -n 20 -P 8 process_job"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "make 2>&1 | tee build.log | grep -i error",
            "# watch live, archive everything"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Command Substitution Pitfalls",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "After splitting, glob expansion fires: `*` matches files",
            "Never parse `ls` — use globs or `find -print0`",
            "One odd filename and the loop misfires"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "parse `ls`",
                "lines": [
                  "splits on blanks",
                  "globs wildcard",
                  "breaks silently"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "glob / `-print0`",
                "lines": [
                  "byte-safe",
                  "no extra process",
                  "newline-proof"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Silent Test Breakage"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "[ $(cmd) = yes ]       # empty: error",
            "[ \"$(cmd)\" = yes ]     # quoted: safe",
            "[ x\"$(cmd)\" = xyes ]   # sentinel trick"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Stripped newlines skew `wc -l` line logic",
            "`$((n + $(cmd)))` dies on 2+ tokens",
            "NUL bytes pass through, corrupt logs"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Status Gotchas",
          "items": [
            "`local out=$(cmd)` always returns 0",
            "Split it: `local out`, then assign",
            "`set -e` won't fire inside `$()`",
            "`if ! out=$(cmd)` detects failure"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "if ! out=$(grep -q pat f); then",
            "  echo \"grep failed, rc=$?\"",
            "fi"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Size and Self-Match"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`$()` as args can exceed `ARG_MAX`, ~2 MB",
            "Pipe big data instead of expanding it",
            "`ps | grep me` matches the grep itself",
            "Use `pgrep -f` or `grep [m]e`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Habit Rule",
          "text": "Treat every `$()` as untrusted text: quote it, bound its size, check its status explicitly."
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Why did `set -e` ignore my failed `$(cmd)`?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "The substitution itself always succeeds",
            "The child status is discarded",
            "Assign on its own line, then test `rc`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "A Filename That Streams",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "`<(cmd)` runs `cmd` and hands back a path that reads its output. Diff two live streams in one line."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "diff <(sort a.txt) <(sort b.txt)",
            "paste <(ls d1) <(ls d2)",
            "while read u; do :; done < <(who)"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Under the Hood"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`/dev/fd/63` — a file descriptor in disguise",
            "No `/dev/fd`? Bash builds a temporary FIFO",
            "Pure kernel pipes: zero temp files on disk"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "<(cmd)",
              "pipe",
              "/dev/fd/63",
              "reader"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Directions"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`<(cmd)` exposes `cmd` stdout for reading",
            "`>(cmd)` gives a writable path into stdin"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "cmd | tee >(gzip > out.gz)",
            "# >(...) = writable pipe end"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Process Substitution Mechanics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Timing Hazards",
          "items": [
            "Substitutions start immediately, in background",
            "Main command may finish before sinks drain",
            "Their `stderr` appears late, interleaved",
            "Add `wait` or a final `cat` into the sink"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "grep pat f >(report)",
            "wait $!    # bash 4.4+: real status"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Limits"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Not seekable: `stat`, `mmap`, seek all fail",
            "`bash`/`ksh`/`zsh` only — `/bin/sh` needs FIFOs"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Anonymous FIFO",
          "text": "Process substitution is a nameless pipe with a borrowed filename. Concurrent, diskless, gone when the command ends."
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Why does my tool choke on `<(cmd)`?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "It tried to `stat` or seek",
            "FIFO-backed paths aren't real files",
            "Use `mktemp` for random access"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "mkfifo: A Named Pipe",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`mkfifo p` makes a directory entry, type `p`",
            "`ls -l` shows `prw-r--r--`, size 0",
            "Pipe semantics wearing a filename"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "mkfifo /tmp/pipe",
            "producer > /tmp/pipe &",
            "consumer < /tmp/pipe",
            "rm /tmp/pipe"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Blocking Is the Feature"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Open for read blocks until a writer opens",
            "Vice versa: a lone open hangs the script",
            "`<>` opens both ways — no peer needed yet"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "producer &",
              "FIFO 64 KB",
              "consumer"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Flow Control",
          "items": [
            "Kernel buffer: 64 KB on Linux",
            "Reader falls behind, writer blocks",
            "Writes ≤ `PIPE_BUF` (4096 B) are atomic",
            "Keep protocol messages under 4096 bytes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Lifetime Rules"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A FIFO outlives its creator — `trap EXIT` unlinks",
            "Many readers split the data between them",
            "Many writers interleave at chunk granularity"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "mkfifo p",
            "trap 'rm -f p' EXIT",
            "job > p &   # peer opens, both unblock"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Where FIFOs Win"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Producer-consumer handoff between scripts",
            "Request channel for a listening daemon",
            "Single-instance job feeds"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
