      "page_title": "Feeding Loops From Commands",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Bulk Loading"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`mapfile -t lines < f`: array, no subshell",
            "No per-line fork, newlines stripped",
            "`for f in $(cmd)`: only deliberate word lists"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Stdin Eaters",
          "items": [
            "`ssh`, `ffmpeg`, prompts swallow loop stdin",
            "Pass `-n` to `ssh`, or `< /dev/null`",
            "`read -t 2` adds a timeout",
            "`read -u 3` reads another descriptor"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Throughput",
          "text": "A `while read` loop forks nothing per line but parses in shell. On big inputs, `mapfile` or `awk` is markedly faster."
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Why is my counter still zero?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "Pipe put the loop in a subshell",
            "Redirect: `done < f` or `< <(cmd)`",
            "Or `mapfile` into an array first"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Pipe Anatomy",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Kernel byte-stream buffer: read fd, write fd",
            "No records, no boundaries — ordered bytes only",
            "`/dev/fd/N`: window onto the descriptor table"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
